encies to fall  into error  not personal lives under the influence  of our emotions for example our minds  are seldom free of the influence of  moods a kind of emotional whether that  scuds over our mental horizons normally  without us having any understanding of  where these moods have come from when  they might lift or even that they exist  however these moods can have a decisive  impact on our ideas we might in one mood  consider ourselves fortunate look  brightly at the future and feel grateful  to those around us and then a few hours  later without anything in the outer  world haven't changed another mood might  lead us to a wholesale re-evaluation of  almost everything about us devilishly  part of what it means to be subject to a  mood is not to realize that we are in  its grip we simply feel that our friends  whom we like quite a lot yesterday and  no good in our job which once offered us  so much is absurd tiredness can be a  particularly powerful agent that  silently and invisibly perverts our  judgment the 19th century skeptic  Friedrich Nietzsche remarked when we are  tired we are attacked by ideas we  thought we'd conquered long ago though  crucially is extremely rare and  counterintuitive to judge that it really  might be tiredness that's affecting our  outlook rather than certain objective  facts in the world we are keener to  conclude that we've suddenly developed a  deep resentment against humanity we  urgently need to get to bed lust can  similarly play with our judgment leading  us to see sensitivity kindness and a  decent alternative to our current  partner where there is in truth just an  exceptionally beautiful profile and  perhaps not much else as the German  sceptical philosopher Arthur  Schopenhauer wryly concluded immediately  after population the devil's laughed  about appreciating how flawed our minds  our forms the basis upon which the score  emotional scepticism is founded this  skill defined is of cautious awareness  of the misleading power of our feelings  on our judgment having survey the  fragilities of our minds  the ancient Greeks sceptical  philosophers recommended that we learn  to develop an attitude of what they  called epoch II translated as reserve or  suspension of judgment aware of our  tendencies to error we were never to  rush into decisions we were to let our  ideas settle so that they could be  re-evaluated at different points in time  and we were to be especially vigilant  about the impact of sexual excitement  and tiredness on the formation of our  plans for a range of historical reasons  we've collectively been extremely  reluctant to recognize the benefits of  emotional scepticism the romantic  movement of the 19th century work we've  dusted a guy Ling but often disastrous  notions that it's our emotions that it's  always our finest guides to the truth  but we will have gone a long way to  counteract the problems of our minds if  we sometimes do ourselves the honor of  not listening to our feelings instead  waiting for certain and helpful moods to  pass and accepting that we are at heart  highly viscous bags of saline solution  who stare out at reality via a highly  unreliable and distorted pane of glass  and must therefore frequently suspend  judgment moderate our impulses who watch  over our diet and strive to get to bed  Darley  you

